        {
          "subsection_id": "partograph_interpretation_subsection_2",
          "subsection_title": "What the station may say",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "partograph_interpretation_group_2",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Examples of OSCE instructions:"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "“Interpret the partograph provided and comment on the progress of labour.”"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "“A 25-year-old primigravida has been in labour for several hours. Assess her partograph and outline your management.”"
                },
                {
                  "type": "checklist",
                  "items": [
                    "“Identify the abnormalities on this partograph.”"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "“The cervical dilatation curve has crossed the action line. What does this mean, and what will you do?”"
                },
                {
                  "type": "checklist",
                  "items": [
                    "“Assess fetal and maternal well-being using the chart provided.”"
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "partograph_interpretation_subsection_3",
          "subsection_title": "Introduction and initial assessment",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "partograph_interpretation_group_3",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "If you are asked to interpret a partograph, begin by checking:"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Patient identification and chart completeness."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Parity and gestational age."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Whether the patient is in spontaneous or induced/augmented labour."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The time labour monitoring began and the cervical dilatation at that point."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Whether the chart uses traditional alert/action lines or the WHO Labour Care Guide."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The timing of the latest maternal and fetal observations."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Any interventions already undertaken, including artificial rupture of membranes, oxytocin administration, analgesia or antibiotics."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "If this is a real patient with suspected obstructed labour, fetal compromise, significant bleeding or maternal instability, urgent clinical assessment takes priority over completing the chart interpretation."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "partograph_interpretation_subsection_4",
          "subsection_title": "Components of a traditional partograph",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "partograph_interpretation_group_4",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "A traditional partograph is generally divided into three major sections."
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/og/partograph_interpretation_04.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "checklist",
                  "items": [
                    "A. Fetal condition",
                    "Fetal heart rate",
                    "Liquor or amniotic fluid characteristics",
                    "Moulding of the fetal skull"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/og/partograph_interpretation_05.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "checklist",
                  "items": [
                    "B. Progress of labour",
                    "Cervical dilatation",
                    "Descent of the presenting part",
                    "Uterine contractions",
                    "Time and relevant events"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/og/partograph_interpretation_06.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "checklist",
                  "items": [
                    "C. Maternal condition",
                    "Pulse and blood pressure",
                    "Temperature",
                    "Urine output and urine findings",
                    "Drugs and intravenous fluids"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The exact variables and recording intervals depend on the chart version and local protocol."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "partograph_interpretation_subsection_5",
          "subsection_title": "How to interpret each component",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "partograph_interpretation_group_5",
              "group_title": "Step 1: Assess fetal well-being",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "checklist",
                  "items": [
                    "Fetal heart rate"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/og/partograph_interpretation_07.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "In a traditional partograph, fetal heart rate is plotted at the prescribed intervals."
                },
                {
                  "type": "table",
                  "columns": [
                    "Finding",
                    "Interpretation"
                  ],
                  "rows": [
                    [
                      "110–160 beats/min",
                      "Generally normal baseline"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Below 110 beats/min",
                      "Fetal bradycardia; assess urgently"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Above 160 beats/min",
                      "Fetal tachycardia; assess for infection, hypoxia and other causes"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Sudden sustained fall",
                      "Possible acute fetal compromise"
                    ]
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The fetal heart rate should be interpreted alongside the CTG when indicated, the clinical circumstances and any other signs of compromise."
                },
                {
                  "type": "checklist",
                  "items": [
                    "Liquor (amniotic fluid)"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/og/partograph_interpretation_08.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The traditional WHO chart commonly records liquor as follows:"
                },
                {
                  "type": "table",
                  "columns": [
                    "Code",
                    "Meaning"
                  ],
                  "rows": [
                    [
                      "I",
                      "Membranes intact"
                    ],
                    [
                      "C",
                      "Clear liquor"
                    ],
                    [
                      "M",
                      "Meconium-stained liquor"
                    ],
                    [
                      "B",
                      "Blood-stained liquor"
                    ]
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Some charts use slightly different codes."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Clinical significance:"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Clear liquor is generally reassuring but does not exclude fetal compromise."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Meconium-stained liquor requires assessment in context; thick meconium is particularly concerning when accompanied by an abnormal fetal heart rate."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Blood-stained liquor may indicate placental abruption or another source of bleeding and requires urgent assessment."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Intact membranes indicate that the membranes have not ruptured at the time of observation."
                },
                {
                  "type": "checklist",
                  "items": [
                    "Moulding"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/og/partograph_interpretation_09.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Moulding refers to overlapping of the fetal skull bones during labour."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The traditional grading is:"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "0: Bones separated."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "+: Bones just touching."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "++: Bones overlapping but reducible."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "+++: Bones overlapping and not reducible."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Increasing moulding, particularly marked non-reducible moulding accompanied by poor descent and abnormal labour progress, raises concern for cephalopelvic disproportion or obstructed labour."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Moulding alone does not establish obstruction; it must be interpreted alongside contractions, cervical dilatation, descent and the overall clinical picture."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "group_id": "partograph_interpretation_group_6",
              "group_title": "Step 2: Assess cervical dilatation",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/og/partograph_interpretation_10.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/og/partograph_interpretation_11.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "checklist",
                  "items": [
                    "slideshare.net"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Cervical dilatation is recorded in centimetres and plotted against time."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The latent phase involves early cervical change before established active labour."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The active first stage involves more rapid cervical dilatation."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The second stage begins at full cervical dilatation and ends with birth."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The exact definition of the active phase depends on the guideline being used. WHO defines the active first stage as beginning at 5 cm, whereas some older charts and protocols use 4 cm."
                },
                {
                  "type": "checklist",
                  "items": [
                    "Understanding the alert and action lines"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "On the traditional modified WHO partograph:"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Alert line: A reference line traditionally representing cervical dilatation of approximately 1 cm/hour during the active phase."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Action line: Traditionally drawn four hours to the right of the alert line."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Interpretation:"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "A dilatation curve remaining to the left of the alert line suggests progress faster than the reference rate."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "A curve approaching or crossing the alert line prompts closer assessment and review of labour progress."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "A curve reaching or crossing the action line indicates the need for a thorough clinical reassessment and a decision on appropriate management."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Critical exam point: Crossing the alert or action line is not, by itself, an automatic indication for oxytocin or caesarean section. Assess the mother, fetus, presentation, position, contractions, descent and possible obstruction before deciding on intervention."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The traditional partograph's 1 cm/hour reference should not be applied as a universal definition of abnormal labour. The WHO Labour Care Guide uses cervical-dilatation thresholds and other clinical observations rather than a single universal linear alert line."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "group_id": "partograph_interpretation_group_7",
              "group_title": "Step 3: Assess descent of the presenting part",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/og/partograph_interpretation_12.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Descent describes how far the presenting part has moved into the maternal pelvis."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "It may be recorded in fifths of the fetal head palpable abdominally:"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "5/5 palpable: Most or all of the head remains above the pelvic brim."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "4/5 palpable: The head remains largely above the brim."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "3/5 palpable: The head is becoming more engaged."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "2/5 palpable: The head is generally considered engaged."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "1/5 palpable: Only a small portion of the head remains palpable abdominally."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "0/5 palpable: The head is no longer palpable abdominally."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The exact interpretation depends on examination technique and the chart being used."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "What the examiner expects: You should compare descent over time with cervical dilatation and contraction adequacy."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "For example, full cervical dilatation with a persistently high presenting part should prompt assessment for malposition, cephalopelvic disproportion, inadequate contractions or obstruction."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "group_id": "partograph_interpretation_group_8",
              "group_title": "Step 4: Assess uterine contractions",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/og/partograph_interpretation_13.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Contractions are recorded according to their frequency and duration."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Traditional partographs often represent the number of contractions in a 10-minute period using shaded boxes to indicate duration."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "A common traditional key is:"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Dots: contractions lasting less than 20 seconds."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Diagonal shading: contractions lasting 20–40 seconds."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Solid shading: contractions lasting more than 40 seconds."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Check the legend on the actual chart, because conventions may vary."
                },
                {
                  "type": "table",
                  "columns": [
                    "Finding",
                    "Clinical interpretation"
                  ],
                  "rows": [
                    [
                      "Infrequent, weak contractions",
                      "May contribute to slow labour progress"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Regular, effective contractions",
                      "Usually support cervical dilatation and descent"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Excessive contraction frequency",
                      "Risk of reduced fetal recovery time and fetal compromise"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Strong contractions with poor progress",
                      "Assess for malposition, obstruction or cephalopelvic disproportion"
                    ]
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Never diagnose inadequate uterine activity from the partograph alone. Clinical assessment is necessary, and intrauterine pressure monitoring is used only in selected settings."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "group_id": "partograph_interpretation_group_9",
              "group_title": "Step 5: Assess maternal well-being",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "table",
                  "columns": [
                    "Variable",
                    "What to look for"
                  ],
                  "rows": [
                    [
                      "Pulse",
                      "Persistent tachycardia, which may indicate pain, dehydration, infection or bleeding"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Blood pressure",
                      "Hypotension, severe hypertension or other clinically significant changes"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Temperature",
                      "Fever suggesting possible infection"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Urine output",
                      "Reduced output, which may indicate dehydration or renal compromise"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Urine protein",
                      "May prompt evaluation for pre-eclampsia in the appropriate context"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Urine ketones",
                      "May suggest starvation or dehydration, but must be interpreted clinically"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Drugs and IV fluids",
                      "Oxytocin, analgesia, antibiotics and fluids, including timing and response"
                    ]
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "A partograph is a monitoring tool, not a substitute for a full maternal assessment. Abnormal observations must be acted on even if cervical dilatation appears satisfactory."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "partograph_interpretation_subsection_6",
          "subsection_title": "Worked OSCE examples",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "partograph_interpretation_group_10",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "These are the types of completed charts you may encounter in an examination."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "group_id": "partograph_interpretation_group_11",
              "group_title": "Case A: Normal progress of labour",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "checklist",
                  "items": [
                    "Reassuring example"
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "group_id": "partograph_interpretation_group_12",
              "group_title": "Clinical findings",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Primigravida at 39 weeks."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Cervix progresses from 5 cm to 8 cm over 3 hours."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Fetal heart rate remains between 130 and 145 beats/min."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Liquor is clear."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Contractions are regular and effective."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The presenting part descends progressively."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Maternal pulse, blood pressure and temperature remain satisfactory."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Interpretation: The findings are consistent with progressive labour and no obvious evidence of maternal or fetal compromise in the observations provided."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Management: Continue appropriate monitoring, supportive care, hydration, pain relief according to preference, and reassessment of maternal and fetal well-being."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "group_id": "partograph_interpretation_group_13",
              "group_title": "Case B: Slow cervical dilatation",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "checklist",
                  "items": [
                    "Requires reassessment"
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "group_id": "partograph_interpretation_group_14",
              "group_title": "Clinical findings",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Multiparous woman at term."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Cervix remains at 6 cm for several hours."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Contractions are infrequent."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Fetal heart rate is 140 beats/min."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Liquor is clear."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Maternal observations are stable."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Interpretation: There is slow progress during the active first stage, potentially associated with inadequate uterine activity. However, the duration alone does not establish the diagnosis or cause."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Management: Review the history and timing of examinations, confirm presentation and position, assess descent and moulding, assess contraction adequacy and exclude obstruction. Consider amniotomy or oxytocin augmentation only when clinically appropriate and according to local protocol, with the required fetal monitoring."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "group_id": "partograph_interpretation_group_15",
              "group_title": "Case C: Suspected obstructed labour",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "checklist",
                  "items": [
                    "Obstetric emergency"
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "group_id": "partograph_interpretation_group_16",
              "group_title": "Clinical findings",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Cervical dilatation shows little or no progress."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Strong, frequent contractions are present."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The fetal head remains high."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "There is marked moulding."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Maternal pulse is rising and the woman appears exhausted."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Interpretation: The combination of poor progress, a high presenting part, marked moulding and maternal deterioration raises strong concern for obstructed labour."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Management: Urgent senior obstetric assessment, maternal stabilisation, intravenous access, appropriate blood investigations and crossmatching, fetal assessment, and preparation for definitive management. Do not augment labour with oxytocin when obstruction is suspected. Expedite birth using the safest appropriate route."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "group_id": "partograph_interpretation_group_17",
              "group_title": "Case D: Slow progress with fetal compromise",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "checklist",
                  "items": [
                    "Urgent escalation"
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "group_id": "partograph_interpretation_group_18",
              "group_title": "Clinical findings",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Cervical dilatation has slowed."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The fetal heart rate is persistently 170 beats/min."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Meconium-stained liquor is present."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Maternal temperature is elevated."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Interpretation: There is slow labour progress with fetal tachycardia, meconium-stained liquor and maternal fever. The differential includes intra-amniotic infection and fetal compromise."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Management: Urgent obstetric review, maternal observations, CTG assessment, investigation and treatment of suspected infection, and assessment for expedited birth according to the full clinical picture. Do not delay escalation while waiting for the next scheduled partograph entry."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "partograph_interpretation_subsection_7",
          "subsection_title": "Management of abnormal labour progress",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "partograph_interpretation_group_19",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Use this sequence in the OSCE."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Recognise the abnormality. Identify slow cervical dilatation, lack of descent, abnormal contractions or concerning maternal/fetal observations."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Assess the mother and fetus. Check vital signs, fetal heart rate, CTG where indicated, hydration, pain and bleeding."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Identify the cause. Consider the powers (contractions), passenger (fetal size, presentation and position) and passage (maternal pelvis and possible obstruction)."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Correct reversible factors. Provide appropriate fluids, analgesia and bladder care; treat hypotension or infection when present."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Decide whether intervention is needed. Depending on the findings, this may include amniotomy, carefully supervised oxytocin augmentation or expedited birth."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Document and reassess. Record findings, actions, the response and the agreed plan. Escalate if the condition deteriorates."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "group_id": "partograph_interpretation_group_20",
              "group_title": "The 3 Ps of labour",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "checklist",
                  "items": [
                    "Powers — uterine contractions"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Assess frequency, duration and adequacy. Weak or infrequent contractions may contribute to slow progress."
                },
                {
                  "type": "checklist",
                  "items": [
                    "Passenger — fetus"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Assess fetal size, presentation, position, attitude and descent."
                },
                {
                  "type": "checklist",
                  "items": [
                    "Passage — maternal pelvis and soft tissues"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Consider cephalopelvic disproportion, pelvic abnormalities and obstruction."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "A fourth consideration often taught is the psyche: fear, anxiety, lack of support and exhaustion may affect the woman's experience and ability to cope with labour. Provide respectful support throughout."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "partograph_interpretation_subsection_8",
          "subsection_title": "Questions examiners commonly ask",
